SQL String Functions

SQL String Functions are built-in functions used to work with and manipulate string/text data.

They are useful for:

- Combining strings
- Changing uppercase/lowercase
- Finding string length
- Extracting parts of a string
- Replacing text
- Removing extra spaces
- Reversing strings

---

1. CONCAT()

Definition

"CONCAT()" is used to combine two or more strings into a single string.

Syntax

SELECT CONCAT(str1, str2, ...);

Example

SELECT CONCAT('Data', ' ', 'Base');

Output

Data Base

Practice Query 1

Combine employee name and department.

SELECT CONCAT(emp_name, ' - ', department) AS employee_details
FROM employees;

Practice Query 2

Display employee name and city together.

SELECT CONCAT(emp_name, ' - ', city) AS employee_location
FROM employees;

---

2. UPPER()

Definition

"UPPER()" converts lowercase characters into uppercase.

Syntax

SELECT UPPER(string);

Example

SELECT UPPER('mani');

Output

MANI

Practice Query 1

Display all employee names in uppercase.

SELECT UPPER(emp_name) AS employee_name
FROM employees;

Practice Query 2

Display department names in uppercase.

SELECT UPPER(department) AS department
FROM employees;

---

3. LOWER()

Definition

"LOWER()" converts uppercase characters into lowercase.

Syntax

SELECT LOWER(string);

Example

SELECT LOWER('MANI');

Output

mani

Practice Query 1

Display all employee names in lowercase.

SELECT LOWER(emp_name) AS employee_name
FROM employees;

Practice Query 2

Display city names in lowercase.

SELECT LOWER(city) AS city
FROM employees;

---

4. LENGTH()

Definition

"LENGTH()" returns the length of a string.

Syntax

SELECT LENGTH(string);

Example

SELECT LENGTH('database');

Output

8

Practice Query 1

Find the length of each employee name.

SELECT emp_name, LENGTH(emp_name) AS name_length
FROM employees;

Practice Query 2

Find employees whose name has more than 5 characters.

SELECT emp_name, LENGTH(emp_name) AS name_length
FROM employees
WHERE LENGTH(emp_name) > 5;

---

5. REPLACE()

Definition

"REPLACE()" replaces a specific part of a string with another string.

Syntax

SELECT REPLACE(str, old_text, new_text);

Example

SELECT REPLACE('mani', 'a', '@');

Output

m@ni

Practice Query 1

Replace Hyderabad with HYD.

SELECT emp_name, REPLACE(city, 'Hyderabad', 'HYD') AS city
FROM employees;

Practice Query 2

Replace IT with Information Technology.

SELECT emp_name,
       REPLACE(department, 'IT', 'Information Technology') AS department
FROM employees;

---

6. LEFT()

Definition

"LEFT()" returns a specified number of characters from the beginning of a string.

Syntax

SELECT LEFT(string, count);

Example

SELECT LEFT('divya', 3);

Output

div

Practice Query 1

Display the first 3 characters of every employee name.

SELECT emp_name, LEFT(emp_name, 3) AS first_3_characters
FROM employees;

Practice Query 2

Display the first 2 characters of every city.

SELECT city, LEFT(city, 2) AS first_2_characters
FROM employees;

---

7. RIGHT()

Definition

"RIGHT()" returns a specified number of characters from the end of a string.

Syntax

SELECT RIGHT(string, count);

Example

SELECT RIGHT('divya', 2);

Output

ya

Practice Query 1

Display the last 3 characters of every employee name.

SELECT emp_name, RIGHT(emp_name, 3) AS last_3_characters
FROM employees;

Practice Query 2

Display the last 2 characters of every city.

SELECT city, RIGHT(city, 2) AS last_2_characters
FROM employees;

---

8. SUBSTR()

Definition

"SUBSTR()" is used to extract a specific part of a string starting from a given position.

Syntax

SELECT SUBSTR(str, start, length);

Example

SELECT SUBSTR('mani', 2, 2);

Output

an

How it works

m a n i
1 2 3 4

Starting from position "2" and taking "2" characters:

a n

So the output is:

an

Practice Query 1

Extract 3 characters from each employee name starting from position 2.

SELECT emp_name,
       SUBSTR(emp_name, 2, 3) AS extracted_text
FROM employees;

Practice Query 2

Extract the first 4 characters from each department name.

SELECT department,
       SUBSTR(department, 1, 4) AS department_short
FROM employees;

---

9. REVERSE()

Definition

"REVERSE()" reverses the characters in a string.

Syntax

SELECT REVERSE(string);

Example

SELECT REVERSE('sql');

Output

lqs

Practice Query 1

Display every employee name in reverse.

SELECT emp_name, REVERSE(emp_name) AS reversed_name
FROM employees;

Practice Query 2

Display every city in reverse.

SELECT city, REVERSE(city) AS reversed_city
FROM employees;

---

10. TRIM()

Definition

"TRIM()" removes leading and trailing spaces from a string.

Syntax

SELECT TRIM(string);

Example

SELECT TRIM('  text  ');

Output

text

Practice Query 1

Remove unnecessary spaces from employee names.

SELECT emp_name, TRIM(emp_name) AS trimmed_name
FROM employees;

Practice Query 2

Remove unnecessary spaces from city names.

SELECT city, TRIM(city) AS trimmed_city
FROM employees;

---

11. SUBSTRING_INDEX()

Definition

"SUBSTRING_INDEX()" returns a part of a string based on a specified delimiter.

Syntax

SELECT SUBSTRING_INDEX(str, delimiter, count);

Example

SELECT SUBSTRING_INDEX('Mani Kumar Reddy', ' ', 1);

Output

Mani

How it works

String:

Mani Kumar Reddy

Delimiter:

' '

Count:

1

It returns the first part:

Mani

Practice Query 1

Extract the first word from "Mani Kumar Reddy".

SELECT SUBSTRING_INDEX('Mani Kumar Reddy', ' ', 1) AS first_name;

Output

Mani

Practice Query 2

Extract the first two words.

SELECT SUBSTRING_INDEX('Mani Kumar Reddy', ' ', 2) AS first_two_words;

Output

Mani Kumar

Practice Query 3

Extract the first word from every employee name.

SELECT emp_name,
       SUBSTRING_INDEX(emp_name, ' ', 1) AS first_name
FROM employees;

---

Quick Reference

Function| Purpose
"CONCAT()"| Combines strings
"UPPER()"| Converts text to uppercase
"LOWER()"| Converts text to lowercase
"LENGTH()"| Finds string length
"REPLACE()"| Replaces text
"LEFT()"| Gets characters from the left
"RIGHT()"| Gets characters from the right
"SUBSTR()"| Extracts part of a string
"REVERSE()"| Reverses a string
"TRIM()"| Removes leading/trailing spaces
"SUBSTRING_INDEX()"| Extracts text using a delimiter

